        "The tenant Lockwood encounters the household at Wuthering Heights and becomes curious about its relationships. Much of the history reaches him through the servant Nelly Dean. Her account begins with the arrival of Heathcliff, a child brought into the Earnshaw family. His closeness with Catherine Earnshaw grows within a household divided by affection, hostility, and social rank.\n\nLockwood’s arrival as a tenant introduces the houses through a visitor who repeatedly misreads their inhabitants. Nelly Dean then supplies a long retrospective account, but her closeness to events does not make her narration disinterested. Heathcliff enters the Earnshaw family as an outsider favoured by the father and resented by Hindley. After the father’s death, Hindley degrades him, while Catherine’s attachment to him exists beside her attraction to the social position represented by Edgar Linton. The landscape and the two houses make these differences tangible. Wuthering Heights and Thrushcross Grange are not simply addresses; they organise contrasting forms of comfort, power, exposure, and aspiration."
