        "Attachment becomes a source of injury",
        "Catherine’s relationship with Heathcliff is intense, but she marries Edgar Linton. The decision links feeling with the desire for status and security. Heathcliff leaves and later returns with resources and a determination to avenge the humiliations he has suffered. His actions reach beyond the people who hurt him, drawing marriages, property, and the next generation into his plans.\n\nCatherine’s decision to marry Edgar does not mean that she has ceased to identify intensely with Heathcliff. Heathcliff overhears only part of her explanation and leaves before hearing how she describes their bond. His later return with unexplained wealth gives him new means to act on resentment. His marriage to Isabella Linton becomes an instrument of cruelty rather than a reconciliation between families. Catherine’s decline and death do not end the conflict. Heathcliff continues to treat living people as means of pursuing a grievance that no practical acquisition can repair. The violence extends beyond romantic rivalry into property, education, inheritance, and the power to determine other people’s daily lives."
