        "After Catherine’s death, Heathcliff’s obsession continues. The lives of young Cathy, Linton Heathcliff, and Hareton Earnshaw become entangled in coercion and inherited resentment. Education, dependence, and control of property shape the available choices. The narrative’s indirect structure matters: the reader receives events through people whose sympathy and judgement are not wholly reliable.\n\nThe younger Catherine, Hareton Earnshaw, and Linton Heathcliff inherit the consequences of choices made before they could understand them. Heathcliff uses debt and marriage to gain control of both properties, deprives Hareton of education, and forces younger Catherine into an unhappy marriage. The possibility of change emerges through a different relationship: Catherine and Hareton move from humiliation and hostility towards shared learning and affection. Their growing companionship contrasts with the older generation’s possessiveness. Heathcliff eventually loses interest in maintaining his revenge and dies preoccupied with Catherine’s presence. Reports of the pair wandering the moors remain uncertain, leaving the ending poised between an ordinary future for the young and an unresolved haunting of the past."
