        "Pip grows up with his sister and her husband Joe, a blacksmith whose kindness provides an important emotional foundation. An encounter with the escaped convict Magwitch begins one strand of the plot. Visits to Miss Havisham and her adopted daughter Estella begin another. Pip becomes painfully aware of class distinctions and imagines that becoming socially refined might make him worthy of Estella.\n\nPip’s first act of assistance to an escaped convict is performed under fear, but it establishes an obligation whose significance he cannot yet understand. His life with Joe, a blacksmith, offers affection and practical work. Visits to Miss Havisham’s decaying house introduce a different measure of value. Estella’s contempt makes Pip ashamed of his manners and station, while Miss Havisham encourages the emotional damage her own betrayal has helped create. When the lawyer Jaggers announces that an anonymous benefactor will finance Pip’s life as a gentleman, Pip interprets the news through the romantic explanation he already wants to believe. His expectation of marrying Estella shapes how he understands almost everything that follows."
