        "Madame Defarge’s determination extends beyond punishing the original perpetrators. The novel shows how a demand for justice can become a drive to destroy a whole family. Carton, whose life has seemed wasted, makes preparations that give him a decisive role. Miss Pross also acts to protect Lucie. Personal courage is placed against institutions whose momentum can become difficult to resist.\n\nA document written by Manette during his imprisonment reveals the crimes of the Evrémonde brothers and implicates the family from which Darnay descends. Madame Defarge’s own relationship to the victims makes her pursuit of the family intensely personal. Lucie and her child become targets despite their innocence of those earlier acts. Miss Pross’s confrontation with Madame Defarge protects their departure, while Carton uses the resemblance established at the first trial to replace Darnay in prison. His sacrifice fulfils a promise to Lucie without turning political terror into a merely private misunderstanding. The imagined future at the close gives his death meaning through lives that can continue, placing deliberate care against the inherited demand that suffering must reproduce itself."
