        "An image changes the way a young man sees himself",
        "The artist Basil Hallward paints Dorian Gray and invests the portrait with unusual personal significance. Lord Henry Wotton introduces Dorian to a way of speaking about beauty, youth, pleasure, and influence. Dorian wishes that the picture might age while he remains young. The supernatural arrangement makes that wish consequential, turning an image into a record of a life that its owner can conceal.\n\nBasil Hallward paints Dorian because he sees an extraordinary beauty that has become central to his art. Lord Henry Wotton supplies another influence: he turns pleasure, youth, and the fear of ageing into seductive arguments. Dorian’s wish that the portrait should bear time’s effects while he remains unchanged makes the painting a record he can hide from public judgement. The arrangement separates appearance from consequence, but only superficially. Dorian’s attraction to the actress Sibyl Vane initially combines love with admiration for her performances. When love changes her relationship to acting and she performs badly, his reaction exposes how readily he confuses a person with the aesthetic pleasure she provides."
