        "Experience becomes something to consume",
        "Dorian’s attachment to the actress Sibyl Vane ends cruelly when her performance no longer gives him the experience he desires. Her death and the portrait’s change establish the gap between his visible appearance and his conduct. Under Lord Henry’s influence and through his own choices, he increasingly treats other people and experiences as material for personal sensation rather than relationships carrying obligations.\n\nDorian’s rejection of Sibyl is followed by her death and the first visible alteration in the portrait. He recognises a connection between his conduct and the painted image, yet Lord Henry helps him reinterpret the tragedy in terms that protect his appetite for experience. As years pass, Dorian cultivates beautiful objects, specialised tastes, and a social existence in which rumours of damaged lives coexist with admiration of his face. The hidden portrait becomes both evidence and fascination. Its disfigurement does not automatically reform him; secrecy allows him to treat moral recognition as something he can repeatedly postpone. Basil’s concern offers an opportunity for confession, but Dorian instead shows him the picture and kills him."
