        "As years pass, Dorian’s appearance protects him from some of the judgements that would otherwise follow his behaviour. The hidden portrait becomes more disturbing. Basil’s confrontation with the truth leads to his murder. Dorian’s efforts to contain the consequences draw other people into danger and deepen the separation between the reputation he can present and the history he cannot bear to see.\n\nAfter the murder, Dorian coerces Alan Campbell into disposing of the body, extending the damage to another person. Sibyl’s brother James presents the threat of an earlier wrong returning in material form, although Dorian’s unchanged appearance initially makes identification seem impossible. Even when that threat disappears, the portrait continues to defeat Dorian’s attempts to see himself as purified. A supposedly generous decision does not restore its beauty, forcing him to confront the self-regard that may accompany his gestures. His final attack on the painting kills the body it has protected, restoring the portrait and exposing his age. The supernatural ending gives visible form to a problem the society around him has repeatedly refused to see."
