        "After Sir Charles Baskerville dies, a legend about a supernatural hound gives his death a terrifying explanation. Dr Mortimer brings the case to Sherlock Holmes as the heir Sir Henry prepares to take his place. The setting on Dartmoor, the family history, and a warning delivered to the new heir create an atmosphere in which ordinary facts can be interpreted through fear before they are fully examined.\n\nDr Mortimer introduces the case through a family manuscript and the circumstances of Sir Charles Baskerville’s death. The story of a spectral hound provides a ready explanation before the investigators have examined its practical alternatives. Sir Henry, the new heir, arrives in London and encounters warnings, surveillance, and the disappearance of a boot. These apparently small incidents are not decorative puzzles: they show preparation for an attack that must depend on locating and identifying its target. Holmes sends Watson to Devon while seeming to remain in London. The resulting distance places readers beside an observer who is competent and loyal but cannot yet see how every strand of the case connects."
