        "Watson accompanies Sir Henry and reports on the household and its neighbours. Strange movements, a fugitive, and the apparently secretive conduct of the Barrymores complicate the case. The naturalist Stapleton and the woman introduced as his sister become important figures. Watson’s work provides observations, but the novel also makes clear the difference between collecting details and understanding the purpose connecting them.\n\nAt Baskerville Hall, the landscape makes uncertainty physical. The moor contains isolated dwellings, dangerous ground, and the sounds and reports that sustain the legend. The Barrymores’ secret assistance to an escaped convict first appears to explain suspicious movements but belongs to a different history. Watson’s contact with Stapleton and the woman presented as his sister introduces another set of misleading relationships. His reports to Holmes record genuine observations while allowing him to make provisional mistakes. Discovering Holmes on the moor reveals that the investigation has continued under conditions Watson did not know about. The concealment changes the meaning of Holmes’s apparent absence without making Watson’s work unnecessary."
