        "Victory comes from an overlooked dependency",
        "The Martians die from earthly microorganisms to which they lack resistance. Humanity survives through a biological circumstance rather than military superiority. The ending brings reunion and relief, while leaving the narrator psychologically unsettled. The book’s comparisons with imperial conquest invite readers to question the confidence of societies accustomed to imagining themselves as the conquerors rather than the conquered.\n\nThe curate’s inability to remain quiet threatens the concealed observers, and the narrator’s desperate response exposes the moral damage of prolonged fear. Later, the artilleryman offers a grand plan for human survival underground. The apparent energy of his proposal gives way to doubts about its discipline, practicality, and authoritarian assumptions. When the narrator finally encounters the dead Martians, the reversal does not arrive as a reward for this plan or for superior strategy. The invaders have neglected an environmental condition that the people they conquered largely took for granted. Recovery therefore includes gratitude without restoring the narrator’s earlier confidence. His reunion with his wife is real, but memory interrupts the renewed appearance of normality. The novel ends with survival understood as contingent, and with the possibility that another world may again refuse the reassuring stories humanity tells about its own security."
