        "Power fails to remove dependence",
        "Griffin’s attacks bring a pursuit that ends in his death, when his body becomes visible again. His notebooks remain an unsettling possibility rather than a reassuring resolution. The novel uses a fantastic premise to examine isolation, ambition, and the practical limits of apparent power. Griffin’s vulnerability is inseparable from the society he wants to intimidate and control.\n\nThe proposed partnership makes that distinction urgent. Griffin wants to use invisibility to impose fear through exemplary violence, rather than merely secure a cure or recover his lost papers. Kemp’s cooperation with the authorities turns him into a target. The pursuit depends on recognising that the invisible body still occupies space, can be struck, and leaves evidence through contact with the environment. Griffin’s eventual death makes his body visible, reversing the transformation that seemed to promise escape from common limitations. The closing reference to his notebooks keeps the discovery from disappearing with him. Marvel possesses something he cannot fully understand, and the possibility of repetition remains. Wells therefore provides neither a simple warning against learning nor a celebration of progress: the danger emerges through the combination of technical power, unexamined entitlement, and the refusal to accept obligations towards other people."
