        "The laws deserve a critical reading",
        "Kipling’s animal communities create memorable patterns of speech and conduct. Their hierarchies can also reflect the assumptions of British imperial culture. Readers can enjoy the storytelling while questioning who defines a rule, who benefits from obedience, and how outsiders are represented. The original collection differs substantially from later film adaptations and contains episodes that those versions omit.\n\nThe non-Mowgli tales broaden the collection rather than simply providing minor diversions from his story. Rikki-tikki-tavi’s position inside a human household gives his encounters with the cobras a domestic stake. Kotick’s search in The White Seal is directed towards finding a place beyond human slaughter, making persistence and discovery alternatives to a conventional battle for supremacy. Toomai’s encounter with the elephants opens a world whose meaning is not exhausted by the human work imposed on them. Stories of animals in military service add another perspective on discipline and interdependence. The poems around the tales change voice and rhythm, reminding readers that the volume was designed as a collection with varied forms. Its recurring concern is how creatures act within communities under pressure, but the answers differ enough that no single slogan about obedience or courage adequately substitutes for the individual stories."
