    "topic": "Conscience and citizenship come into conflict when institutions sustain injustice.",
    "description": "Conscience and citizenship come into conflict when institutions sustain injustice.",
    "symbol": "❧",
    "guideFormat": "Extended guide",
    "sections": [
      [
        "The essay questions obedience as a moral default",
        "Often known as Civil Disobedience, this essay examines the relationship between individuals and government. Thoreau argues that majority rule and legal authority do not automatically establish justice. His discussion is rooted in opposition to slavery and the Mexican-American War, rather than a purely abstract preference for personal independence.\n\nThoreau begins by challenging the assumption that government becomes morally authoritative simply because it can organise force or secure the agreement of a majority. A person may obey efficiently while failing to examine whether the action carried out is just. The essay distinguishes the operation of a political machine from the exercise of conscience. Slavery and the war with Mexico give that distinction an immediate historical urgency: injustice is being sustained through institutions that ordinary citizens support in different ways. The argument does not require readers to imagine themselves as innocent observers outside all social arrangements. Instead, it asks where their own participation enters those arrangements and whether disapproval expressed only in conversation leaves the practical support untouched. That is why the essay repeatedly moves from an opinion about government to a question about what an individual actually does."
      ],
      [
        "Responsibility includes the support people provide",
        "Thoreau describes refusing a poll tax and spending a night in jail. He asks whether people who oppose an injustice can remain morally detached while continuing to support institutions that carry it out. His emphasis is on personal participation and refusal, though his account does not resolve every question about collective organisation or political effectiveness.\n\nThe refusal to pay the poll tax becomes Thoreau’s particular answer, not a universal procedure capable of resolving every injustice by itself. His account of a night in jail reverses the apparent meaning of confinement. The state can place his body behind a wall, but he presents that power as inadequate to settle the moral disagreement. Descriptions of the cell, his fellow prisoner, and the town outside keep the episode concrete. The experience also reveals a difference between how an institution treats a person and how that person comes to understand the institution. Thoreau is less interested in portraying himself as physically triumphant than in exposing what he sees as a confusion between coercion and legitimate judgement. The unusual perspective asks readers to reconsider the location of freedom without pretending that imprisonment has no practical consequences."
      ],
      [
        "A provocative argument requires contextual judgement",
        "The essay has influenced later discussions of nonviolent resistance, but it should not be reduced to permission to ignore any inconvenient law. Readers can distinguish conscience from mere preference, examine the effects of resistance on others, and compare the essay’s individual emphasis with the strategies used by later movements. This overview describes a historical argument, not legal advice.\n\nThe later reflections test the limits of reform through existing political procedures. Thoreau questions whether voting, petitioning, and waiting for a majority can satisfy conscience when serious harm continues in the meantime. He also considers the danger that a person’s economic attachments can make principled refusal seem impossible. His emphasis on individual responsibility gives the essay much of its force, while leaving questions that later collective movements would answer differently. It does not supply a complete theory of organising, protection, or the distribution of risk among participants. Nor does its conclusion simply celebrate disorder. Thoreau imagines a more just political relationship in which the state recognises the individual as a source of moral authority. A responsible reading holds the historical demand for resistance beside the need to judge actions, consequences, and obligations carefully in their own circumstances."
      ]
    ],
