        "A self-portrait is not an independent verdict",
        "This autobiography was published after Carnegie’s death. It represents his perspective and priorities, so readers should compare it with other histories of labour, industrial power, and the Homestead conflict. The account can illuminate how a prominent industrialist understood his life without requiring acceptance of every judgement he makes about success, responsibility, or the origins of his fortune. His reflections on international peace and public service also reveal a desire to shape a legacy beyond business. Readers can compare these aspirations with the consequences of industrial practices described in other sources.\n\nThe later movement towards philanthropy supplies Carnegie with a way of describing wealth as an obligation as well as an achievement. Libraries, educational institutions, research, and public causes become vehicles through which private resources are intended to support opportunities beyond the donor’s lifetime. The idea raises practical questions about who chooses a need, who maintains an institution, and how access is distributed once a gift has been made. Carnegie’s interest in peace also extends his ambitions into international public life, where wealth and personal conviction do not automatically produce the influence he hopes for. Read as a whole, the autobiography is therefore a constructed account of a legacy: it connects an immigrant’s early work with a wealthy man’s explanation of what his fortune should mean. Its value increases when admiration for particular contributions can coexist with scrutiny of the economic and social conditions through which the power to make those contributions was accumulated."
