        "White Fang eventually travels south to California, where he learns to live with Scott’s household. His protection of the family and the arrival of puppies complete the movement towards domestic belonging. The story contains violence and period stereotypes, and its account of animal psychology is imaginative. Its central reversal asks how changed treatment can allow a creature defined by fear to behave differently. In this respect the novel reverses the direction of The Call of the Wild: its movement is towards attachment to a human household, rather than away from domestic life into wilderness.\n\nThe journey to California places White Fang in another unfamiliar social environment, one with household routines, domestic animals, and expectations he has not previously had a reason to learn. His attachment to Scott helps him adapt, but mistakes and uncertainty remain part of the transition. The family must also revise its view of him rather than wait for him alone to do all the understanding. His defence of the household against Jim Hall gives his strength a protective role within the community. Serious injuries then return him to dependence on care, and the final scene with puppies offers an image of continuity shaped by safety rather than fighting. The direction deliberately contrasts with Buck’s movement in The Call of the Wild. Neither novel supplies a universal animal-behaviour rule. Their paired imaginative question concerns what an environment rewards, what fear teaches, and what different forms of attachment make possible."
