        "Visits, conversations, and changing expectations expose the vanity of Anne’s family and the complexity of the people around her. The accident at Lyme disrupts assumptions about romantic attachments. Anne’s practical usefulness and emotional steadiness contrast with the emphasis others place on rank, appearance, and fashionable connections.\n\nThe Musgrove circle introduces opportunities for companionship alongside further misunderstandings. Wentworth’s attention to Louisa and Henrietta can be read as evidence of a new attachment, while Anne must participate in conversations without claiming the significance their shared past gives them. At Lyme, Louisa’s fall disrupts a mood in which firmness and enthusiasm had seemed uncomplicated virtues. Anne’s calm usefulness becomes visible at a moment when social performance matters less than the ability to act. The consequences of the accident alter expectations about Louisa’s future and reveal the danger of treating observed attention as a settled promise. Other relationships also challenge quick reading. The Crofts’ companionship provides a model of marriage shaped by shared experience, in contrast with the Elliot household’s preoccupation with how a connection will appear to observers."
