        "A disagreement over the view",
        "Lucy Honeychurch arrives at a pension in Florence with her older cousin Charlotte Bartlett, who is responsible for chaperoning her. Their rooms lack the view they expected, and Mr. Emerson offers to exchange rooms with them. His practical generosity sounds intrusive to people accustomed to a more elaborate etiquette. The dispute introduces a central contrast in the novel. Small material comforts and acts of kindness become hard to accept when a social world has trained its members to read every exchange through the possibility of obligation or impropriety.\n\nLucy is curious and capable of strong feeling, particularly when she plays the piano. She is also accustomed to relying on other people’s accounts of what an experience should mean. The pension’s visitors supply numerous competing views of Italy and of one another. Miss Lavish’s enthusiasm for observing life and Charlotte’s concern for appearances provide different examples of how ready-made stories can take the place of attention to the person or place in front of them."
