        "Back in England, Lucy becomes engaged to Cecil Vyse. Cecil is educated and confident in his taste, but he often responds to Lucy through an idea of the refined woman she ought to represent. Her family’s familiar life at Windy Corner gives the reader a sense of what those ideas fail to encompass. When the Emersons take a nearby cottage, the separation Lucy hoped to preserve between Italy and England becomes impossible. The young people’s ordinary meetings allow an experience she had tried to classify as finished to return in a different form.\n\nCecil reads aloud from a novel by Miss Lavish, and Lucy recognises an episode derived from what happened in Italy. Charlotte has shared a story Lucy expected to remain private. George’s response helps make the engagement harder to sustain, while Lucy’s decision to end it rests on her recognition that Cecil does not see her as an independent person. Ending the engagement is one decision. Admitting what she wants from George requires a further change."
