        "Lucy tries to avoid admitting her attachment, including through plans to travel away from the people involved. Mr. Emerson speaks to her plainly about the consequences of denying a feeling she already understands. His directness is effective in part because the reader has seen how much energy has been spent on explanations that preserve appearances while obscuring what is actually happening. Lucy and George ultimately return to Florence together. Their relationship brings freedom and happiness, while leaving tensions with family and social expectations to be acknowledged.\n\nThe view of the title gains meaning through this sequence. It begins as something promised by a room and becomes a way of asking how a person gains access to a wider understanding. Forster’s comedy keeps that change tied to small habits of speech and judgement. A wider view can start with accepting a generous offer, noticing how another person actually behaves, or recognising that an impressive account of good taste has left very little room for the life someone is trying to live."
