        "A Wall Street lawyer describes the copying office in which he employs several clerks. He presents himself as prudent and mild, someone who has found a comfortable way of managing people and business. Turkey and Nippers have weaknesses the lawyer can organise around, while the office boy Ginger Nut occupies a familiar place in its routine. Bartleby arrives as a quiet, pale man whose initial output is impressive. A screen gives him a position close enough to be called when needed while keeping him partly out of sight.\n\nThe physical details matter because the lawyer understands people through the arrangement of his office. Each worker has characteristic habits that can be tolerated if the office remains productive. Bartleby seems manageable at first because he produces the copies he is hired to make. His response to a request to help check the work then introduces a kind of difficulty that cannot be accounted for by the known irritabilities of the other clerks."
